Macular degeneration
Geriatric & Frailty
Painless central visual loss, Distortion of straight lines (metamorphopsia), Preservation of peripheral vision, Drusen visible on fundoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe distress following the sudden, rapid worsening of central vision and extreme distortion of sight in one eye over the past 48 hours.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with known mild visual issues who experienced a sudden, dramatic loss of central vision and severe distortion (straight lines appearing wavy). Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for the acute development of "wet" (neovascular) macular degeneration, which requires immediate ophthalmological intervention to prevent permanent, irreversible central blindness prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "When I look straight at the door frame, it looks completely bent and wavy, and there is a dark smudge right in the middle of my vision."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a history of gradual, painless, and progressive loss of central visual acuity over the past two years, previously diagnosed as "dry" macular degeneration. Over the last 48 hours, they experienced a sudden, alarming worsening in the right eye. They describe severe distortion of straight lines, causing them to appear distinctly wavy or bent (metamorphopsia). They note the rapid development of a dense central blind spot (scotoma) that completely obscures faces or reading material when looking directly at them. They confirm the absolute preservation of their peripheral vision, allowing them to navigate the room without tripping. They have historically had difficulty adapting to low light levels. The current sudden, severe vision loss is highly suggestive of choroidal neovascularization (the "wet" variant). They deny any eye pain, flashes of light, or a "curtain" falling over their vision.
Functional Status: Normally independent; currently highly anxious and struggling to perform tasks requiring fine central vision (e.g., reading the phone).
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to acute depth perception issues; severe anxiety regarding impending blindness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Highly anxious, frequently covering the right eye to see clearly with the left.
Physical Exam: External eye exam is completely normal; no conjunctival injection, clear corneas, and equal, reactive pupils (no afferent pupillary defect). Confrontation visual field testing confirms the preservation of peripheral vision bilaterally. Visual acuity testing (using a modified bedside chart) shows a profound drop in central acuity in the right eye compared to baseline. Amsler grid testing is strongly positive in the right eye, with the patient drawing severe distortion (metamorphopsia) of the grid lines and a central scotoma. Direct fundoscopy is challenging without dilation, but drusen (small yellow subretinal deposits) are suspected in the macular region. (Subretinal fluid visualized on OCT is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding requiring urgent specialist imaging).
Environmental Observations: The home is well-lit, but the patient is struggling to identify specific medications due to the central scotoma.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications currently taken are known to cause acute macular toxicity.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Acute Neovascular ("Wet") Age-Related Macular Degeneration (Right Eye) - Status: Acute Ophthalmological Emergency
Differentials:

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a classic "curtain falling" over the visual field, sudden flashes of light (photopsia), or a dense shower of new floaters; the peripheral vision remains entirely intact.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of severe ocular pain, headache, nausea, vomiting, or a cloudy/steamy cornea.

Transient Ischemic Attack (Amaurosis Fugax) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The vision loss is persistent and characterized by distortion (metamorphopsia) rather than a temporary, complete monocular black-out that resolves within minutes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of permanent severe visual impairment and falls)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic 'red flag' symptoms for acute "wet" macular degeneration: sudden onset of severe metamorphopsia (distortion of straight lines) and a dense central scotoma, with preserved peripheral vision. Telephone triage correctly identified this as a potential time-critical event. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have delayed the urgent specialist referral necessary for sight-saving anti-VEGF intraocular injections. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the clinical confirmation of acute metamorphopsia via Amsler grid testing, ruling out other acute painless visual loss emergencies (like retinal detachment), and facilitated the immediate, urgent coordination of an ophthalmology review for the following morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassured the patient that they are not going completely blind, as peripheral vision is preserved. Generated an urgent, marked referral to an ophthalmologist or the local rapid-access eye clinic for immediate assessment and likely Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) in the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict "patch and prepare" safety routine tonight. Step 1: The patient lightly patches the affected right eye tonight when moving around the house to eliminate the disorienting, distorted visual input, relying solely on the stable left eye. Step 2: Upon successfully navigating to the kitchen using this method, the patient rewards themselves with a calming cup of tea. Cause-and-effect: Patching the acutely distorted eye immediately reduces the sensory conflict that causes dizziness and falls. Rewarding the deliberate use of this safety strategy reinforces compliance and directly reduces the severe anxiety associated with the sudden visual change.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated; ophthalmology is the priority.
DME/Safety (Optional): Amsler grid provided for ongoing daily self-monitoring of the left (unaffected) eye.
Follow-up: Urgent ophthalmology review mandatory within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient develops sudden, severe pain in the eye, if the vision goes completely black, or if they experience a sudden shower of flashes and floaters.

PATIENT INFORMATION

